\subsection{The objectives of level generators}

We define an objective function $o$ for a given generator as a weighted sum of components corresponding to validity $v$, reliability $r$, and intra-generator diversity $d$ of a batch of levels $L$ produced by the generator for a given game.


\subsubsection{Validity}\label{sec:validity_Only}

The validity term captures how well the batch of generated levels conform to soft constraints required by the game and each game has different validity constraints. For example, in Sokoban and Zelda a desired constraint is the level forming one connected region. To encourage this constraint we count the number of connected components present in the level and penalize generators for each additional region formed. In Zelda, valid levels contain $[2,5]$ enemies. We penalize how far outside the range a generated level falls. We give a full description of all validity constraints and penalties in the supplementary material.

Once we aggregate all validity penalties into a negative penalty term $v_l$ for a given level $l$ in batch $L$, we average all penalties to form the validity term $v$:

% A level's \textit{validity} corresponds to the extent to which it satisfies user-defined playability constraints and/or heuristics of quality or complexity, called level \textit{metrics}. It is given by:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:validity}
\begin{split}
  v &= \frac{1}{|L|}\sum_{l\in L} v_l
\end{split}
\end{equation}

%where $v_l$ is a penalty reflecting the (in)validity of a level $l$ in terms of the sum of the absolute differences between the target $g_m$ and current value $s_{m,l}$ along each level metric $m\in M$. Our target along some metric can be an interval (e.g. between 2--5 enemies in zelda), so we generalize, and, of a set of targets $g_m\in G_m$ along some metric, we take that with minimum distance from the current value. The validity penalty of a \textit{batch} of levels is the mean level validity of that batch.

% The static targets are different for each game. However, in all cases they specify the requirements for a valid level. For example in Zelda the static targets include having one avatar, one door, one key, and a single connected room. An ideal score is zero as there would be no difference between the target and actual statistic of the level. A valid, playable level has a \(p_l\) score of zero.

A perfectly valid level will obtain $v_l = 0$, while invalid levels will have a negative $v_l$ term.

%A perfectly valid level will have $v_l = 0$. Note that perfect validity is not a prerequisite for playability: while a game level here must always have at most 1 player avatar, other metrics, intended as heuristics measuring complexity or difficulty (e.g. the distance of the nearest enemy to the player in zelda) need not be maximal for a level to be playable.
%Whenever any of the latter such metrics are taken as \textit{measures} for the archive of elite generators, they are, naturally, excluded from the calculation of $v$.

%Let the batch targets penalty be \(p\) and \(L\) be the set of all batch levels. Formula ~\ref{eq:003_batchstatic} gives the targets penalty for each batch. A batch of all valid levels will have a score of zero.

%\begin{equation}\label{eq:003_batchstatic}
%p = \sum_{l \in L} p_l
%\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Reliability}

The reliability term captures the precision a generator maintains in measurement space. The batch of levels $L$ produced by the generator may have different outputs for the measure functions. For example, in the maze domain the length of the maze solution is a measure function for the QD problem. In this case, reliability captures how consistently a generator can produce levels of a given length.

%The reliability of a generator reflects the consistency with which it produces the same measure values over a batch of generated levels. This in turn allows for an archive of generators that is controllable along measures of interest with a high degree of precision.

For each measure $m_i \in M$, the reliability penalty $r_i$ is the standard deviation of the batch of levels $L$ along measure $m_i$. We then take the average reliability score over the set of measures to arrive at the generator's reliability penalty $r$:

% The reliability penalty assigned to a given generator over a batch of levels corresponds to the mean standard deviation of the batch of levels $L_i$ along measures of interest $i \in I$ (which may comprise some of the functional level metrics that would otherwise be included in $M$ in Equation \ref{eq:validity}, and/or other, aesthetic level features such as emptiness or symmetry):

\begin{equation}\label{eq:reliability}
%r = - \frac{1}{|I|} \sum_{i \in I} \text{std} (L_i)
r = - \frac{1}{|M|} \sum_i r_i
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Diversity}

Intra-generator diversity refers to the amount of variation among the batch of levels produced by a generator. The term discourages generators from collapsing to a single optimal level, ignoring latent seed inputs.

%julian make clear that this is "intra-individual" diversity in the results of a single generator, not the diversity among generator

The diversity bonus $d$ is the mean pairwise hamming distance over the batch of levels, normalized per-tile; or the frequency (as a ratio from 0--1) with which a given coordinate tile differs between any two levels:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:diversity}
d = \frac{1}{(h \cdot w \cdot |L|^2)-1} \sum_{i \in L} \sum_{j \in L} H(i,j)
\end{equation}

where \(w\) and $h$ are the width and height of the levels, respectively, and \(H(i,j)\) is the hamming distance between levels \(i\) and \(j\) (viz. the number of cells on the level-grid that are not occupied by the same type of tile on both levels).

\subsubsection{Combining Terms}\label{sec:combo_obj}
Finally, we combine all three terms into a weighted sum to form the objective function $o$:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:objective}
o = v + \text{max}(0, r + 10 d)
\end{equation}

To understand the weighting of the terms, first consider how reliability and diversity affect one another. The desired goal of a generator is to produce a distribution of valid levels with precise measure values. One issue is a generator can obtain trivial diversity by having low reliability. So unreliability should detract from the diversity bonus, but not the validity score. Note that the output range of reliability is approximately $[-50, 0]$ and the diversity output range is a ratio in the range $[0, 1]$. We weight diversity to be closer to the range of reliability and then take the maximum with 0. The term $\max(0, r + 10 d)$ ensures that the reliability penalty only contributes when diversity is high enough. Finally, we add the $\max(0, r + 10 d)$ term to the validity penalty term to form our final objective function to be maximized in the QD problem.

